Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

devops.workflows.* covers the Actions RUN lifecycle. Everything else GitHub
was missing: the MCP could watch a build but not see the release it
produced, not read a PR, not set the secret a workflow needs.

31 tools under devops.gh.*, every one taking an optional `repo` (the
app/APK workflows and releases live in the unix monorepo, not cloud):
  PRs        list, view (with check rollup), diff, create, comment,
             review, merge, close, ready
  Issues     list, view, create, comment, close, reopen
  Releases   list, view (with ASSETS — which APK a tag currently carries),
             create, upload, download, delete
  Artifacts  run_artifacts, artifact_download
  Repo       repo_view, branch_list, tag_list, commit_list
  Secrets    secret_list, secret_set, secret_delete
  Escape     api — generic `gh api`, the catch-all for anything not wrapped
             (deployments, environments, checks, collaborators, webhooks)

FIXES A LATENT HANG IN THE SHARED exec LIB. execAsync spawned with stdin as
a pipe and never wrote or closed it, so any child reading stdin blocked
until the timeout killed it. stdin is now always ended, and both exec and
execAsync accept an optional `input`. Note the path: c3-infra-mcp's
src/code/shared is a SYMLINK into c3-infra-api, so this file is shared
between the two services — the fix benefits both, and both should be
redeployed together.

SECRET VALUES NEVER ENTER argv. `gh secret set NAME --body VALUE` puts the
value in the process list where anyone with `ps` on the host can read it.
The value goes on stdin instead (hence the exec fix), is never logged, and
is never echoed back — secret_set returns only the name and ok/failed.
secret_list is documented as names-only because GitHub genuinely cannot
return values.

DESTRUCTIVE OPERATIONS REQUIRE confirm=true: pr_merge, pr_close,
issue_close, release_delete, secret_delete, and any non-GET `api` call.
These tools sit behind ONE bearer token valid until 2036, stored in
plaintext, alongside devops.docker.exec and devops.vm.reset. An accidental
merge or secret deletion is a real failure mode; an explicit flag makes
intent deliberate without blocking anything actually intended.

Validation is about FLAG injection, not shell injection — execAsync passes
an argv array so there is no shell, but gh reads a leading "-" as an
option. Repos, numbers, secret names and api field names are pattern
checked, and positional values may not begin with "-". Large outputs
(diffs, api responses) are tailed to 300 lines.

Typechecked against the real dependency tree: 9 pre-existing errors before
and after, none in the new files. This module's safeRun annotates its
callback return types, so unlike the copies in workflows/finops/vps-ops it
does not contribute a TS2322.
